#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "4f2ec8a1-0ab4-55ea-9472-2dd7b9fa5f8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13151" :question/problem "For the sets $A = \\{3,4,7\\},$ $B = \\{f,g\\},$ and $C = A \\times B,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(f,4) \\in C$\n\n- $(7,g) \\in C$\n\n- $(3,f) \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $A \\times B$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/683bad9c822cea7218b2fd8a252e46215103c95b3ee9c42e41e7141ed8a40330.png)\n\nHence, the set $C = A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\big\\{ \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(3,f)}, (3, g), (4, f), (4, g), (7, f), \\bbox[2pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(7,g)}\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "0a101406-0053-5507-8989-3c1cd6e8d4e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13152" :question/problem "If $A = \\{ p,q \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4,9 \\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A \\times B) \\times B?$\n\n- $((p,4),9)$\n\n- $((q,9),4)$\n\n- $(p,4,9)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A \\times B$ consists of every combination of $\\{ p,q \\}$ with $\\{ 4,9 \\},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/69ff8391a11b7bfa221b6b303f31551288bd969bdeed778fb05d3b7094e027ae.png)\n\nHence, the set $A \\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\{ (p,4), (p,9), (q,4), (q,9)\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A \\times B| = |A| \\cdot |B| = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A \\times B) \\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A \\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/5f4a74a98f9df15273d59131eadc1155ff2d1105e1027e7d10b7e2366102e046.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A \\times B) \\times B| = |A \\times B| \\cdot |B| = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that both $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((p, 4), 9)}$ and $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}((q,9),4)}$ are in $(A \\times B) \\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88fb08f1-03f1-5b04-8b48-4b153e522040" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "677fc67f-94df-57a7-a804-22337acda702" :question/math-academy-id "e-13154" :question/problem "If $C = \\{ 3, 4, 7, 9, 11 \\}$ and $D  =\\{ 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 10 \\},$ what is the cardinality of $C \\times D?$" :question/worked-solution "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C \\times D$ is\n\n$$\n| C \\times D | = |C| \\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054351 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc77b3d-fca6-5f66-ac12-e9fedcce15bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13160" :question/problem "Construct a schematic of the set $A^2$ for $A = \\{0,3 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The set $A^2$ is equivalent to the Cartesian product $A^2 = A \\times A.$\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times A$, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $A$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/4646235c2009dc45d7d09a52162ec0bdf929d122f450791ae9917b3ad8ef5a43.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of the first set and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of the second set.\n\nOur completed $A \\times A$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8c9647361885913d2534fefb49a878c2e365cf5be36d1bb4c059d95344dd1a60.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6128eca-7f76-5386-9d67-d81db2943eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff854629-36a3-5955-bf04-2c127465fd5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307252} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9a53b4-f1b5-5d43-a1e2-215aea8438a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307253} {:answer/id #uuid "bca5f009-c31f-513a-bd20-9ce30d84d101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307254} {:answer/id #uuid "6fef36c2-2000-5321-80c7-9f1c5258df02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307255} {:answer/id #uuid "eb210385-b0c7-513e-8bf9-bd63fdad1a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fef36c2-2000-5321-80c7-9f1c5258df02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307255} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d181f6-10e5-595f-bc2d-7ee82d906f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61b61c9d-7c11-5ceb-9276-f403a6310963" :question/math-academy-id "q-1261" :question/problem "For the sets $X={x,y,z},$ $Y={v,w},$ and $Z=X\\times Y,$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(x,v)\\in Z$\n- $(y,w)\\in Z$\n- $(w,z)\\in Z$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $X\\times Y$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $X$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $Y.$\n\nIn this case, the schematic for $X\\times Y$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/316a12102e4be81291f06403815cfb63749b1fd3f463c465294e031543123484.png)\n\nHence, the set $Z=X\\times Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nZ={(x,v),(x,w),(y,v),(y,w),(z,v),(z,w)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bddf2eba-66b3-5c57-8971-e666025619ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051828} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f0b17d-052c-58c5-9dff-e4e0edb4e0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051829} {:answer/id #uuid "0351d24f-f991-5fa0-b241-d2546268d4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051830} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd262ed-54aa-582e-8311-18cae3226f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051831} {:answer/id #uuid "5c60a479-57a0-5395-bd89-9b7f2e52c3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c60a479-57a0-5395-bd89-9b7f2e52c3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051832} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c6ef9f-def6-5951-ac83-3c2ba842b19b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59457bab-d41c-5c4d-af91-59c9dc67dad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181294" :question/problem "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfea42e3-6937-5f27-bade-4da516d3026f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051834} {:answer/id #uuid "fb752239-abfd-5869-81ef-a126b7b80c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051835} {:answer/id #uuid "0202ec4a-d1fe-56c2-9780-1a6ed209364b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051836} {:answer/id #uuid "0c964175-10c4-57d2-9c42-dde9a9c2736e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051837} {:answer/id #uuid "58a32f4e-971c-526b-a7de-6ce91581dc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb752239-abfd-5869-81ef-a126b7b80c8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051835} :answer-field/id #uuid "d151bfaf-5293-5de2-bb7f-d9f14b99da44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9eac2d72-9d93-50cd-8b4e-008064a9d365" :question/math-academy-id "q-181295" :question/problem "If $A={-1,1}$ and $B={u,v},$ which of the following elements belong to $B\\times B\\times A?$\n\n- $(v,v,1)$\n- $((v,v),1)$\n- $(1,v,u)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ consists of all ordered triples whose first coordinate belongs to $A,$ second coordinate belongs to $B,$ and third coordinate belongs to $C.$\n\nIn this case, $B\\times B\\times A$ consists of every combination of ${u,v}$ with ${u,v}$ and ${-1,1},$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\times B\\times A={ & (u,u,-1),(u,u,1),(u,v,-1),(u,v,1), \\\\  & (v,u,-1),(v,u,1),(v,v,-1),(v,v,1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n|B\\times B\\times A|=|B|\\cdot |B|\\cdot |A|=2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $(v,v,1)\\in B\\times B\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27990870-efbc-53e4-837c-87bef46ea3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/63d6365a037294d0ceb4f320b86b16ce2c70011a8c5660ba7cbb283179026091.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307280} {:answer/id #uuid "00daf33b-a9e8-5721-ab65-af8ae1c1bf55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/6aee614cc6259f0f9d84d25764a325320faa55419f848943dfd92dca50160cd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307281} {:answer/id #uuid "4cef9860-5872-53dd-9a71-1dfb6f92c8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/5e14edf4c5890bc213d9a488b9135e142a369ea6ee0259cc183a523b7c5316e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307282} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5550b5-9b8b-5163-b3c3-6623536597eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/88fe1481ff4c18a96f93d452bf8624851049c1b378504728a94eaac354321a9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307283} {:answer/id #uuid "6bba6e4a-86f2-5776-a469-4a02ac6a6aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/1afafe8de19ab9a48d7be7cbe8ff66840008b642595a731c58e687e96cef49f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cef9860-5872-53dd-9a71-1dfb6f92c8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/5e14edf4c5890bc213d9a488b9135e142a369ea6ee0259cc183a523b7c5316e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307282} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b725afb-5a2d-5d2c-a6d3-9276add4c764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3bc5496-8327-5fd1-912a-4e5c3716ac24" :question/math-academy-id "q-181957" :question/problem "Which of the following is a schematic of the Cartesian product $A\\times B$ for $A={-1,1}$ and $B={x,y,z}?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo create a schematic of the Cartesian product, we create a table and list the elements of the first set $A$ in a row across the top and the elements of the second set $B$ in a column down the left side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/8fe661f4daa7a5bb75dea993f46bbf8970400a798e23da4018d3e4a0b891273d.png)\n\nThen, at each intersection, we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B.$\n\nOur completed $A\\times B$ schematic is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/49270545404b21e8068b35cdb59c756e1f3bc3c22608359c8d7e83389e38c7a4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b304527c-5cdc-57df-b118-e555f6f8fa5c" :knowledge-point/title "Creating a Schematic for a Cartesian Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710c12de-5d01-5b08-bbc9-ef2f623d067e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307287} {:answer/id #uuid "788ab1c9-a77b-5ebe-9ea8-c085ff8a685f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307288} {:answer/id #uuid "b8255dae-9506-5229-829b-b36f76b3e4e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307289} {:answer/id #uuid "6519ab84-a21e-5fcd-a8e6-ab23a33f8df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307290} {:answer/id #uuid "97963324-1fd9-5c84-9ade-883794dce552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "710c12de-5d01-5b08-bbc9-ef2f623d067e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307287} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33d4708-7849-502c-b078-0d0aa6a56353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5132636e-fc5c-596d-8624-67ad2af0f5ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-181963" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/cb3e6ef6a832f2adbbc5e096bddf2c4f6a0445fbd77a71df5617298f8dad2e51.png)\n\nBy finding the missing entries in the schematic shown above for $A\\times B,$ where $A={a,b,c}$ and $B={2,4,6},$ determine which of the following statements are true.\n\n- $(4,b)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B$\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nTo complete our schematic of $A\\times B,$ we write an ordered pair whose first coordinate is the corresponding element of $A$ and the second coordinate is the corresponding element of $B$ at each intersection.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040355/assets/57d053cca53046f02201540e655030a37ba8f7a2811e7dc724cf30ca9fd3c072.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram:\n\n- $(4,b)\\notin A\\times B\\,\\times $\n\n- $(c,6)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\n- $(a,2)\\in A\\times B\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
